How to Remove Stuck Filament from PTFE Tube
Fortunately, it is not difficult to solve this problem. Cleaning the pipe manually is usually enough to make the 3D printer run again.
What causes filament to get stuck in the PTFE tube ?
If the filament is not used for a long time, the filament has a sufficient opportunity to absorb moisture. The next time you print with it, it may become brittle and break easily and causes the filament to get stuck in hotend
As for the filament stuck in the short PTFE tube of the heater, there may be other reasons, such as thermal creep or the gap between the tube and the metal part of the heater
There are a few things you can do to prevent the filament from breaking and getting stuck:
A bend with a small radius will generate more friction than a bend with a large radius. So whenever possible, make sure that the path of the tube is not too constrained.
Ensure that the filament spool can roll freely.
2.Remove the collet from the Bowden coupling. This is a typical blue, red or black clip that looks a bit like a horseshoe.
One of the most common reasons for the filament to get stuck in the heat exchanger is that the PTFE tube cannot reach the heat interrupter or nozzle. This creates a gap where the filament can melt and expand and causes PTFE tube stuck in hotend.
1、Unscrew the nozzle partially. This loosens the thermal insulation device on the other end of the heater block.
If you cannot unscrew the screw by hand, you can use two thin M6 nuts to tighten at one end. Then, you can use the inner nut of the wrench to unscrew the screw of the heat protector.
5、Pull out the tube from the other end. You may need to use some tool to push it in from the top
7、Reassemble the hotend. Make sure that the lamp is consistent with the heat interrupter (or nozzle, depending on the heater design) so that no molten filament escapes to unwanted places.
What if you can’t push the filament out?
It is inconvenient to stick the filament on the Bowden tube or heater, but this is not the end of the world. With a little careful disassembly and cleaning, you can make your extruder restart and run in no time
